include "../../linux.mereo"

-- A DEFINITION written inside another definition. Refused here, at the
-- declaration, rather than silently becoming something else.
--
-- Definitions do not nest: a namespace (`NAME contains`) is what holds them, and
-- a definition holds methods and fields. But `rec is` reads exactly like a
-- method with no parameters, so the parser took it as one -- `grp.rec` became a
-- callable method whose body declared a local, nothing called it, and a
-- procedure method is otherwise only checked when it is INLINED. So nothing ever
-- looked at it again. The mistake surfaced at the USE:
--
--   h is buf as grp.rec
--   ^  mereoc: error: `as` needs a view (signed/unsigned/big/little/...)
--
-- which names neither the nesting nor the group.
--
-- What makes it checkable: a method taking no parameters reaches the world in
-- exactly two ways -- it calls something, or it writes its resource's state. A
-- body of nothing but declarations of its own locals does neither, so it cannot
-- have an effect however it was meant. Declaring an INSTANCE is not inert
-- (`tests/scopes/04_method_local` acquires one in a method precisely for the
-- release), which is why that case is excluded.
--
--   refused:  'rec' takes no parameters and its body only declares names

grp is
  rec is                             -- meant as a view; parsed as a method
    tag is 1 bytes
  end

  go (value) is
    value is 7
  end
end

program is
  n is 0

  grp.go (value is n)

  ensure n == 7
end
